From b2a7741cd881e5ed1cc8af10c48795a66a2c6a23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: joris-sta <joris-sta@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:45:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] server: retention memory budgets, checkpoint anchor
 authority, atomic tool-call publication

Persistent resume store disk budgets: --resume-max-mib plus an adjacent
--resume-budget-path directory folded into accounting by recursive,
symlink-safe byte counting. Eviction frees only the store's own entries,
never external files; unreadable roots are treated as "no evidence",
not as license to evict. make_room() protects live slot ids and
prefers retained favorites, and free_bytes() clamps to the budget.

Context checkpoint authority: a checkpoint may only replace the earliest
representative of a mandatory turn bucket when replay-boundedness and
retention-turn scoring prove coverage anchoring
(server_cache_checkpoint_anchor_covered); the attempt latch tracks task
ids so consecutive requests of the same task reuse lanes correctly.
Large MTP checkpoints no longer enlarge the displaced host capture
budget (--checkpoint-max-mib). /props-style metadata gains a
cache_identity (token count + retention token digest + sequence epoch)
for cross-restart lineage checks at cache planning.

Streaming tool calls are atomic for the PEG-native parser: partial
parses no longer retract published SSE prefixes or expose half-parsed
tools; native tool calls are held until a strict final parse and
published in one diff, malformed final tool requests degrade to a text
notice instead of aborting the stream, and the qwen3-coder
parameter-framing grammar can no longer swallow subsequent calls after
a mismatches closing tag (delimiters kept outside values so constrained
sampling must close each parameter).

Reasoning budget: a prefill that already contains a closed thinking
block ends the budget before any forced message, instead of leaking the
forced message into the final answer (RFC committed with regression
tests). CLI shows generated-token counts from server timings.
